' HUB75_INSTRUMENT turns on the refresh cog's strobes (P8-P11) and the monitors of
' isp_hub75_instrument.spin2. It is defined only in test top files such as this one, and
' exported so that every child object sees it.
#DEFINE HUB75_INSTRUMENT
#PRAGMA EXPORTDEF HUB75_INSTRUMENT

{ What this program is:

   The measuring top file of FRAME-RATE sprint section 2. It starts adapter 1 (DISP0_) through the
   normal display API, starts the instrument for that adapter, and runs the same workload on it:
   a flat fill, lines, text, a BMP, each followed by a commit. After each commit it samples the
   monitors for SAMPLE_SECONDS windows and prints, per phase, the refresh, the lit share of /OE,
   the CLK mean rate and high time, the draw time and the commit time.

   The colour depth is a compile-time setting (DISP0_COLOR_DEPTH in isp_hub75_hwPanelConfig.spin2),
   so one build measures one depth. Build it once for each depth 3 to 8 and compare the logs.
   The line printed first names the depth.

   After the phases it runs the frame-set handshake trace (FRAME-RATE plan section 3): 100 commits
   back to back, each printed with the frame set it converted into and the set on display, a FAIL
   line for any commit that converted into the set on display, and a HANDSHAKE PASS/FAIL verdict;
   then it calls display.showFrameSet(0) and prints whether the call was refused.

   Then the brightness steps (full, half, the chip-minimum floor, off), each labelled on the
   panel with its step, its brightness and the lit share measured relative to full, so a viewer
   can judge the dimming by eye (on a display too narrow for a label line, the steps run
   unlabelled and the log says so); then the tearing test (white and black alternating at the
   fastest commit rate). The plan section 1 test patterns run first, right after start, so a
   viewer sees them at once: drawn through the display API and held PATTERN_HOLD_SECONDS each
   (grey ramp, RGB bands, solid red, green, blue and white, one-pixel checkerboard). The
   frame-set take check runs from the first pattern to the end of the tearing test: every take must find the frame's last row address on the address pins (a
   frame boundary), and the takes counted must equal the commits made. TAKE CHECK PASS/FAIL.

   Right after start, before anything is drawn, the colour-table self-test (FRAME-RATE plan section 8)
   runs: for every depth 3 to 8, gamma off and on, it builds the table into a scratch table with
   colorUtils.fillColorTable() and compares all 256 entries with referenceStored(), a copy of the old
   per-pixel function; then it compares the adapter's live table, read through correctedSingleColor()
   for red, green and blue, at the gamma setting it was built with. Every mismatch is printed;
   COLOUR TABLE PASS/FAIL. Falsification: build with  pnut-ts -D HUB75_CORRUPT_COLOUR_TABLE  and
   colorUtils.fillColorTable() writes one wrong entry into every table it builds, so every case must
   report FAIL.

   Next, still before anything is drawn for viewing, the row-run self-test (FRAME-RATE plan section 9).
   It lays the adapter's panels out three ways (its own wiring, an L three cells wide, an L three cells
   tall; the two Ls only on an adapter of exactly four panels, and the log says when they are not
   run), each at all four display rotations and with all four arrows, through the instrument-only
   hub75Bffrs.testRelayout(); the build's panel configuration is not changed. On each layout it first
   compares screenUtils.pixelAddressAtRC() with oldPixelAddress(), a copy of the per-pixel address rule
   as it stood before row runs, at the display's edges, cell edges and a lattice inside. Then, for each
   case, it draws the case pixel by pixel (screenUtils.setPerPixelReference(TRUE)) on a background
   fill, records every long that differs from the background, draws the case again by runs on the
   same background, and compares the whole screen buffer byte for byte. A drawing that changes more
   longs than the record holds is compared in windows: drawn again for each window, the window
   copied whole. Line, column and box cases run on every layout; text, scrolling, fills, the BMP and
   the 7-segment segments run on one arrow per layout and rotation (12 layouts). Each case prints
   PASS, or FAIL with the bytes that differ and the first differing offset; ROW RUN PASS/FAIL. The
   adapter's own layout and font are put back and the screen buffer cleared. Falsification: build with
   pnut-ts -D HUB75_CORRUPT_ROW_RUN  and screenUtils' run step is one byte short, so every run case
   must report FAIL (the address-rule lines do not use the step and still PASS); build with
   pnut-ts -D HUB75_CORRUPT_PIXEL_ADDRESS  and pixelAddressAtRC() answers one pixel early, so every
   address-rule line must report FAIL.

   Then the scroll oracle self-test: a short text scrolled along a whole text line, left and right
   in SCROLL_FOREVER and left in SCROLL_ONCE_TO_CLEAR, is compared at character boundaries with the
   same characters drawn as static text, an independent drawing; SCROLL ORACLE PASS/FAIL.
   Falsification: build it against the scroller as committed before 09 Oct 2026 and all 15 frames
   must report FAIL (a window's last column left undrawn, the text wrapped only once, the window
   drawn before its mode was set).

   With the adapter at base P0 the instrument reports its monitors unavailable (the strobes would
   be that adapter's colour lines) and this program prints only draw and commit times.
}

CON ' ---- Timing ----

    CLK_FREQ                = 335_000_000                   ' system freq as a constant
    _CLKFREQ                = CLK_FREQ                      ' set system clock

    DEBUG_COGS              = %11111111
    DEBUG_BAUD              = 2_000_000

CON ' ---- Workload ----

    ' Monitor windows taken after each commit; the figures reported are the last window's
    SAMPLE_SECONDS          = 4

    ' Lines drawn in the lines phase, and how many colours they alternate between
    LINE_COUNT              = 16
    LINE_COLOR_PHASES       = 2

    ' The phases of one pass, in order:
    '  PHASE_FILL  - one flat colour over the whole display
    '  PHASE_LINES - LINE_COUNT lines fanned out from the top-left corner
    '  PHASE_TEXT  - lines of text from the top-left corner
    '  PHASE_BMP   - the BMP file loaded into the screen buffer
    '  PHASE_COUNT - number of phases
    #0, PHASE_FILL, PHASE_LINES, PHASE_TEXT, PHASE_BMP, PHASE_COUNT

    FIRST_ROW               = 0                             ' top row of the display
    FIRST_COLUMN            = 0                             ' leftmost column of the display
    FIRST_TEXT_COLUMN       = 0                             ' leftmost text column

CON ' ---- Frame-set handshake trace ----

    ' Back-to-back commits traced (no drawing between them: the fastest commit rate)
    TRACE_COMMITS           = 100

    ' First commit of the trace: it has no earlier trace commit to compare with
    FIRST_TRACE_COMMIT      = 0

CON ' ---- Brightness steps and tearing test (visit C) ----

    ' Monitor windows taken at each brightness step (one per second, long enough to judge by
    ' eye); the step reports the last one
    BRIGHTNESS_SAMPLE_SECONDS = 5

    ' Brightness steps measured, in order (BRIGHTNESS_STEP_COUNT entries in brightnessSteps)
    BRIGHTNESS_STEP_COUNT   = 4

    ' The steps' brightness values: full, FULL_BRIGHTNESS / BRIGHTNESS_HALF_DIVISOR, the chip-minimum
    ' floor, and off
    BRIGHTNESS_HALF_DIVISOR = 2
    BRIGHTNESS_FLOOR        = 1
    BRIGHTNESS_OFF          = 0

    ' Seconds the tearing test alternates two full-screen patterns at the fastest commit rate
    TEAR_TEST_SECONDS       = 20
    MS_PER_SECOND           = 1_000

    ' After the last commit, wait this long for its post to be taken before reading the take
    ' check: longer than one refresh at any rate above 10 Hz
    TAKE_SETTLE_MS          = 100

CON ' ---- Test patterns (plan section 1), judged by eye ----

    ' Seconds each pattern is held
    PATTERN_HOLD_SECONDS    = 6

    ' Patterns, in the order shown:
    '  PATTERN_GREY_RAMP   - grey 0..255 left to right across the display
    '  PATTERN_RGB_BANDS   - four horizontal bands: red, green, blue and white ramps
    '  PATTERN_SOLID_RED .. PATTERN_SOLID_WHITE - every pixel one full colour
    '  PATTERN_CHECKER     - one-pixel white and black checkerboard (dark pixels must stay dark)
    '  PATTERN_COUNT       - number of patterns
    #0, PATTERN_GREY_RAMP, PATTERN_RGB_BANDS, PATTERN_SOLID_RED, PATTERN_SOLID_GREEN, ...
    PATTERN_SOLID_BLUE, PATTERN_SOLID_WHITE, PATTERN_CHECKER, PATTERN_COUNT

    ' The band pattern's bands, top to bottom:
    '  BAND_RED   - the red channel ramps
    '  BAND_GREEN - the green channel ramps
    '  BAND_BLUE  - the blue channel ramps
    '  BAND_WHITE - all three channels ramp
    '  BAND_COUNT - number of bands
    #0, BAND_RED, BAND_GREEN, BAND_BLUE, BAND_WHITE, BAND_COUNT

    ' The colour packing comparison: the packed forms compared, and the blue channel's step
    PACKING_FORMS           = 2                             ' packedCorrectedColor() and packedCorrectedRgb()
    PACKING_BLUE_STRIDE     = 7                             ' an odd stride, so blue follows neither red nor green

    ' Levels in an 8-bit channel
    LEVEL_COUNT             = colorUtils.MAX_COLOR_VALUE + 1

    ' A checkerboard pixel is lit when its row + column is odd
    CHECKER_PHASES          = 2
    CHECKER_LIT_PHASE       = 1

    ' Width or height of a one-pixel box
    ONE_PIXEL               = 1

CON ' ---- Colour table self-test ----

    ' Depths compared (the scratch tables cover every depth whatever the build's depth)
    TABLE_DEPTH_FIRST       = hub75Bffrs.DEPTH_3BIT
    TABLE_DEPTH_LAST        = hub75Bffrs.DEPTH_8BIT

    ' Gamma cases, in order: off, on
    GAMMA_CASE_OFF          = 0
    GAMMA_CASE_ON           = 1
    GAMMA_CASE_COUNT        = 2

CON ' ---- Scroll oracle self-test ----

    ' Each case scrolls ORACLE_TEXT on text line ORACLE_LINE across the whole line, which holds the
    ' text more than twice on every panel width this driver runs, and compares the screen buffer with
    ' the same characters drawn as static text (display.setColoredTextAtLnCol()): an independent
    ' drawing, so a frame wrong in both scroller paths alike is still seen. A case is compared on the
    ' steps whose frame lies on a character boundary.
    '  ORACLE_LEFT_FOREVER  - scrolling left in SCROLL_FOREVER: the text repeats along the line
    '  ORACLE_RIGHT_FOREVER - scrolling right in SCROLL_FOREVER
    '  ORACLE_LEFT_CLEAR    - scrolling left in SCROLL_ONCE_TO_CLEAR, the mode set after the start
    '  ORACLE_CASE_COUNT    - number of cases
    #0, ORACLE_LEFT_FOREVER, ORACLE_RIGHT_FOREVER, ORACLE_LEFT_CLEAR, ORACLE_CASE_COUNT

    ORACLE_LINE             = 1                             ' text line the cases scroll on
    ORACLE_FIELD_PIX        = 6                             ' 5x7 font: 5 pixel columns and a 1 pixel gap
    ORACLE_RIGHT_LEAD       = 5                             ' a right scroll first lies on a boundary after this many steps
    ORACLE_STEP_COUNT       = 5                             ' boundaries compared per case (oracleBoundaries)
    ORACLE_MAX_COLUMNS      = 64                            ' longest text line the expected string holds
    HASH_ROTATE             = 5                             ' bits each long rotates the buffer hash by


    ' Layouts the panels are laid out in for the test:
    '  LAYOUT_OWN        - the adapter's own wiring (DISP0_C0 .. C15), with every arrow replaced
    '  LAYOUT_WIDE_L     - an L three cells wide: C0 C1 C2 along the top, C3 below C0
    '  LAYOUT_TALL_L     - an L three cells tall: C0 C1 C2 down the left, C3 right of C0
    '  TEST_LAYOUT_COUNT - number of layouts
    #0, LAYOUT_OWN, LAYOUT_WIDE_L, LAYOUT_TALL_L, TEST_LAYOUT_COUNT

    ' Panels in each L layout (the adapter must have this many for those layouts to apply)
    L_LAYOUT_PANELS         = 4

    ' Entries in testRotations and in testArrows
    ROTATION_COUNT          = 4
    ARROW_COUNT             = 4

    ' Cases, in the order run. The first FULL_SET_CASES run on every layout; the rest run on one
    ' arrow per layout and rotation (isReducedLayout()):
    '  ROW_CASE_LINES         - lines inside one cell, across two and three cells, the whole width
    '                           right to left, and one pixel; in the top, second and last cell rows
    '  ROW_CASE_OFF_DISPLAY   - lines that start, end or lie off the display (held to its edges)
    '  ROW_CASE_PANEL_CLIP    - panel-centric lines clipped by each panel's edges
    '  ROW_CASE_COLUMNS       - vertical lines, as ROW_CASE_LINES with the axes exchanged
    '  ROW_CASE_COLUMN_OFF    - vertical lines that start, end or lie off the display
    '  ROW_CASE_COLUMN_CLIP   - panel-centric vertical lines clipped by each panel's edges
    '  ROW_CASE_BOXES         - filled boxes narrow and tall (column runs) and wide (row runs),
    '                           an outline, a one-pixel box, and panel boxes clipped by their panel
    '  ROW_CASE_DIAGONALS     - sloped lines: shallow and steep, both directions, across cells, ends off the display
    '  ROW_CASE_CIRCLES       - circles across a cell corner, inside one cell, and running off the bottom right
    '  ROW_CASE_TEXT_5X7      - text in the 5x7 font, top line and the last line's end
    '  ROW_CASE_TEXT_DITHERED - the same in the dithered 5x7 font
    '  ROW_CASE_TEXT_8X8      - the same in the 8x8 font
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_LEFT   - text scrolling left, 5x7, the region partly below the display
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_RIGHT  - text scrolling right, dithered, the region partly past the right edge
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_UP     - text scrolling up, 5x7, across a cell boundary
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_DOWN   - text scrolling down, dithered, the region partly below the display
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_IN_LEFT  - text scrolling left, 5x7, the region inside one panel (each glyph one block)
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_IN_RIGHT - text scrolling right, dithered, inside one panel
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_IN_UP    - text scrolling up, 8x8, inside one panel
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_IN_DOWN  - text scrolling down, dithered, inside one panel
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_OVERDRAW - text scrolling left inside one panel while a box is drawn over its
    '                             window and the buffer is cleared between steps (the window watch)
    '  ROW_CASE_SCROLL_CLEAR    - text scrolling left inside one panel in SCROLL_ONCE_TO_CLEAR, set after
    '                             the scroll starts, in a window wider than the text (spaces past it)
    '  ROW_CASE_FILL_PANEL    - one whole panel filled (display.fillPanel())
    '  ROW_CASE_FILL_AREA     - layout rectangles across cells and off the layout (fillLayoutArea())
    '  ROW_CASE_BMP           - the BMP placed with a content rotation
    '  ROW_CASE_SEGMENTS      - every 7-segment orientation, and one running off the bottom right
    '  ROW_CASE_COUNT         - number of cases
    #0, ROW_CASE_LINES, ROW_CASE_OFF_DISPLAY, ROW_CASE_PANEL_CLIP, ROW_CASE_COLUMNS, ROW_CASE_COLUMN_OFF, ...
    ROW_CASE_COLUMN_CLIP, ROW_CASE_BOXES, ROW_CASE_DIAGONALS, ROW_CASE_CIRCLES, ROW_CASE_TEXT_5X7, ROW_CASE_TEXT_DITHERED, ROW_CASE_TEXT_8X8, ...
    ROW_CASE_SCROLL_LEFT, ROW_CASE_SCROLL_RIGHT, ROW_CASE_SCROLL_UP, ROW_CASE_SCROLL_DOWN, ...
    ROW_CASE_SCROLL_IN_LEFT, ROW_CASE_SCROLL_IN_RIGHT, ROW_CASE_SCROLL_IN_UP, ROW_CASE_SCROLL_IN_DOWN, ROW_CASE_SCROLL_OVERDRAW, ROW_CASE_SCROLL_CLEAR, ...
    ROW_CASE_FILL_PANEL, ROW_CASE_FILL_AREA, ROW_CASE_BMP, ROW_CASE_SEGMENTS, ROW_CASE_COUNT

    ' Cases run on every layout: those before the first text case
    FULL_SET_CASES          = ROW_CASE_TEXT_5X7

    ' Bands the line and column cases draw in:
    '  LINE_BAND_TOP    - from the first row (column)
    '  LINE_BAND_SECOND - from the first row (column) of the second cell
    '  LINE_BAND_LAST   - from the first row (column) of the last cell
    '  LINE_BAND_COUNT  - number of bands
    #0, LINE_BAND_TOP, LINE_BAND_SECOND, LINE_BAND_LAST, LINE_BAND_COUNT

    ' Lines within each band, as offsets from its first row (column); every offset is inside the
    ' shortest cell
    BAND_OFST_ONE_CELL      = 0
    BAND_OFST_TWO_CELLS     = 2
    BAND_OFST_FULL          = 4
    BAND_OFST_THREE_CELLS   = 6
    BAND_OFST_ONE_PIXEL     = 8

    ' Lines of the off-display cases, and of the clip cases within each panel, as rows (columns)
    OFF_LINE_START_BEFORE   = 1
    OFF_LINE_END_AFTER      = 3
    OFF_LINE_ALL_BEFORE     = 5
    CLIP_LINE_BOTH          = 1
    CLIP_LINE_AFTER         = 3
    CLIP_LINE_BEFORE        = 5
    LINE_BEFORE_PANEL       = -1

    ' Line geometry: a one-cell line stays RUN_INSET in from the cell's edges; a two-cell line
    ' reaches RUN_BOUNDARY_REACH either side of a cell boundary; a three-cell line runs from the
    ' middle of the first cell (CELL_HALVES) to the middle of the third (THREE_CELLS); lines off
    ' the display reach OFF_DISTANCE beyond an edge
    RUN_INSET               = 1
    RUN_BOUNDARY_REACH      = 3
    CELL_HALVES             = 2
    THREE_CELLS             = 3
    OFF_DISTANCE            = 10

    ' Boxes: the narrow box is BOX_NARROW_COLUMNS wide, BOX_INSET from the display's top and
    ' bottom; the wide box is BOX_WIDE_ROWS tall, BOX_INSET from its left and right; the outline is
    ' BOX_OUTLINE_INSET in from every edge; the one-pixel box sits at BOX_DOT_POSITION
    BOX_INSET               = 2
    BOX_NARROW_COLUMNS      = 3
    BOX_WIDE_ROWS           = 4
    BOX_OUTLINE_INSET       = 5
    BOX_DOT_POSITION        = 9

    ' Text: runText starts on the first line; the last line's text starts TEXT_TAIL_CHARS before its end
    FIRST_TEXT_LINE         = 0
    TEXT_TAIL_CHARS         = 3

    ' A box's inset, or a rectangle's reach, applies on both of its sides
    BOTH_SIDES              = 2

    ' Scrolling: the window is SCROLL_WINDOW_CHARS wide (the text is three characters longer, so a
    ' sideways scroll crosses character boundaries); the buffers are compared after
    ' SCROLL_COMPARE_STEPS steps (past one character's width and gap sideways, and through every
    ' row offset of a glyph up and down, ending on a step that moves the frame rather than one
    ' that draws the whole window after a wrap), then the scroll is run to its end (at most
    ' SCROLL_FINISH_STEPS steps) so the scroller is free again. A region partly off the display
    ' starts SCROLL_EDGE_OVERLAP rows (columns) before the edge.
    SCROLL_WINDOW_CHARS     = 3
    SCROLL_COMPARE_STEPS    = 9
    SCROLL_FINISH_STEPS     = 64
    SCROLL_EDGE_OVERLAP     = 3
    SCROLL_CELL_REACH       = 8
    PANEL_P0                = 0                             ' the first panel in reading order, as mounted

    ' The fill-area rectangles: rows and columns past each edge, and their sizes
    FILL_EDGE_REACH         = 2
    FILL_RECT_ROWS          = 6
    FILL_RECT_REACH         = 7
    FILL_CORNER_ROWS        = 8
    FILL_CORNER_COLUMNS     = 20
    FILL_STRIP_ROWS         = 3

    ' The overdraw scroll case: OVERDRAW_STEPS steps between its interruptions; the box is
    ' OVERDRAW_BOX_SIZE pixels square, OVERDRAW_BOX_INSET pixels into the window, in OVERDRAW_COLOR
    OVERDRAW_STEPS          = 3
    OVERDRAW_BOX_INSET      = 2
    OVERDRAW_BOX_SIZE       = 4
    OVERDRAW_COLOR          = $40_FF_40

    ' The clear-mode scroll case: a window CLEAR_WINDOW_CHARS wide, wider than scrollText, so past
    ' the text it shows the text again until SCROLL_ONCE_TO_CLEAR is set, and spaces after
    CLEAR_WINDOW_CHARS      = 9

    ' Sloped lines run DIAG_REACH pixels past the display's edges; the steep ones lean
    ' DIAG_STEEP_RUN columns either side of the first cell boundary
    DIAG_REACH              = 5
    DIAG_STEEP_RUN          = 9

    ' Circles: one of CIRCLE_SMALL_RADIUS inside the first cell, one of CIRCLE_EDGE_RADIUS centred
    ' CIRCLE_EDGE_INSET pixels in from the bottom right, so most of it is off the display
    CIRCLE_SMALL_RADIUS     = 6
    CIRCLE_EDGE_RADIUS      = 9
    CIRCLE_EDGE_INSET       = 2

    ' Segments: placed SEGMENT_REACH before a cell's corner, SEGMENT_SPACING apart; the last one
    ' SEGMENT_EDGE_ROWS above the bottom and SEGMENT_EDGE_COLUMNS before the right edge
    SEGMENT_REACH           = 4
    SEGMENT_SPACING         = 4
    SEGMENT_EDGE_ROWS       = 2
    SEGMENT_EDGE_COLUMNS    = 3

    ' The drawings' color: every channel non-zero and unlike the background byte
    RUN_TEST_COLOR          = $FF_80_40

    ' Comparison: the screen buffer is filled with BACKGROUND_BYTE before each drawing. The longs of
    ' the pixel-by-pixel drawing that differ from it are recorded in captureArea, index and value,
    ' up to CAPTURE_LONGS of them; a drawing that changes more is compared in windows of
    ' CAPTURE_AREA_LONGS longs, each copied whole into captureArea.
    BACKGROUND_BYTE         = $5A
    BACKGROUND_LONG         = $5A_5A_5A_5A
    CAPTURE_AREA_LONGS      = 8192
    CAPTURE_LONGS           = CAPTURE_AREA_LONGS / 2
    BYTE_MASK               = $FF
    NO_OFFSET               = -1                            ' first differing offset when none differ

    ' Address-rule comparison points: one pixel beyond each display edge, every cell edge, and a
    ' lattice of every ORACLE_ROW_STEP-th row and ORACLE_COLUMN_STEP-th column
    ORACLE_EDGE_REACH       = 1
    ORACLE_ROW_STEP         = 5
    ORACLE_COLUMN_STEP      = 7

CON ' ---- Brightness step labels ----

    ' Text lines of the label, above the BMP: the step line, and the measured-lit line under it
    LABEL_STEP_LINE         = 0
    LABEL_LIT_LINE          = 1

    ' Characters in one label line; shorter text is padded with spaces to erase the last label
    LABEL_LINE_CHARS        = 24

    ' The step's monitor window the label's lit figure comes from. The first window was already
    ' running when the brightness changed, so it mixes two steps; the second starts after it.
    LABEL_WINDOW            = 2

    ' Decimal digits are built by repeated division by the base
    DECIMAL_BASE            = 10

    ' Screen-buffer byte values for the tearing test's two patterns (every channel full, or off)
    SCREEN_BYTE_WHITE       = $FF
    SCREEN_BYTE_BLACK       = $00

OBJ

    color       : "isp_hub75_color"
    hub75Bffrs  : "isp_hub75_hwBufferAccess"
    display     : "isp_hub75_display"
    bmpHandler  : "isp_hub75_display_bmp"
    hwEnum      : "isp_hub75_hwEnums"                       ' chip flags
    colorUtils  : "isp_hub75_colorUtils"                    ' color table under test
    matrix      : "isp_hub75_rgb3bit"                       ' constants only: flag mask, long size
    instrument  : "isp_hub75_instrument"                    ' monitors and stopwatches
    pixels      : "isp_hub75_screenUtils"                   ' row runs under test, and the address rule
    user        : "isp_hub75_hwPanelConfig"                 ' the adapter's own wiring sentences
    segment     : "isp_hub75_segment"                       ' 7-segment lines under test

DAT ' ---- Test State (singleton) ----

    textMessage     BYTE    "HUB75 RATES 0123456789",0
    labelLine       BYTE    0[LABEL_LINE_CHARS + 1]         ' label line being built, zero-terminated
    labelLength     LONG    0                               ' characters in labelLine so far
    scrollCaseRegion LONG   0                               ' the scroller an inside-a-panel scroll case started
    bmpFile         FILE    "TstBG-Up.bmp"

    chainIndex      LONG    0                               ' descriptor index of adapter 1
    colorDepth      LONG    0                               ' bit planes per colour
    scratchTable    BYTE    0[LEVEL_COUNT]                  ' colour table built for one depth, to compare
    drawMicros      LONG    0                               ' draw time of the phase just run, microseconds
    commitMicros    LONG    0                               ' commit time of the phase just run, microseconds

    ' brightness steps measured by runBrightnessSteps(): full, half, the chip-minimum floor, off
    brightnessSteps LONG    matrix.FULL_BRIGHTNESS, matrix.FULL_BRIGHTNESS / BRIGHTNESS_HALF_DIVISOR, BRIGHTNESS_FLOOR, BRIGHTNESS_OFF

    ' the handshake trace, one entry per traced commit (see runHandshakeTrace())
    traceConverted  LONG    0[TRACE_COMMITS]                ' frame set the commit converted into
    traceShownAtPost LONG   0[TRACE_COMMITS]                ' frame set on display when that conversion was done
    traceShownNow   LONG    0[TRACE_COMMITS]                ' frame set on display when the commit returned

    ' the row-run self-test (see runRowRunTest())
    testRotations   LONG    bmpHandler.ROT_NONE, bmpHandler.ROT_RIGHT_90, bmpHandler.ROT_180, bmpHandler.ROT_LEFT_90
    testArrows      LONG    hwEnum.ARROW_UP, hwEnum.ARROW_RIGHT, hwEnum.ARROW_DOWN, hwEnum.ARROW_LEFT
    ownSentences    LONG    user.DISP0_C0, user.DISP0_C1, user.DISP0_C2, user.DISP0_C3
                    LONG    user.DISP0_C4, user.DISP0_C5, user.DISP0_C6, user.DISP0_C7
                    LONG    user.DISP0_C8, user.DISP0_C9, user.DISP0_C10, user.DISP0_C11
                    LONG    user.DISP0_C12, user.DISP0_C13, user.DISP0_C14, user.DISP0_C15
    wideLSentences  LONG    hwEnum.FIRST_PANEL, hwEnum.RIGHT_OF | hwEnum.C0, hwEnum.RIGHT_OF | hwEnum.C1, hwEnum.BELOW | hwEnum.C0
    tallLSentences  LONG    hwEnum.FIRST_PANEL, hwEnum.BELOW | hwEnum.C0, hwEnum.BELOW | hwEnum.C1, hwEnum.RIGHT_OF | hwEnum.C0
    testSentences   LONG    0[hub75Bffrs.MAX_CABLE_POSITIONS]   ' the sentences of the layout under test
    ' the reference drawing: CAPTURE_LONGS long indices then their values, or one window of the buffer
    captureArea     LONG    0[CAPTURE_AREA_LONGS]
    runText         BYTE    "RUN 0123456789 ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz", 0
    tailText        BYTE    "END", 0
    scrollText      BYTE    "AB12CD", 0                     ' SCROLL_WINDOW_CHARS + 3 characters
    insideDirections LONG   display.DIR_TO_LEFT, display.DIR_TO_RIGHT, display.DIR_UP, display.DIR_DOWN   ' the inside-a-panel scroll cases, in case order
    emptyNote       BYTE    0                               ' no note after a case line

    ' the scroll oracle self-test (see runScrollOracleTest())
    oracleText      BYTE    "AB12CD", 0                     ' shorter than half of every text line
    oracleBoundaries LONG   0, 1, 3, 8, 13                  ' character boundaries compared: up to twice past the text
    oracleExpected  BYTE    0[ORACLE_MAX_COLUMNS + 1]       ' the line the scroller should show, zero-terminated

DAT ' ---- Debug text (in hub RAM, not in the DEBUG data, which is limited to 16 KB and fails silently near it) ----

    ' Each string is a debug() literal without its first and last characters, which stay inline:
    ' a literal on each side keeps the debugger from putting ", " between it and a value

    dbgHub75Workload             BYTE    "ATES: ---- HUB75 rates workload ---", 0
